\section{Introduction}
Modern high-integrity computational systems face persistent challenges: nondeterministic numerical drift, cryptographic exposure windows, and instability under high-entropy physical inputs. Traditional floating-point solvers introduce architecture-dependent rounding discrepancies, while conventional encryption pipelines expose transient plaintext states to memory buses.

This work introduces a unified deterministic simulation architecture resolving these limitations through the integration of three core components: the Q64.64 fixed-point kernel, the Real-Time Encryption Engine (RT-EE), and the Multi-Layered Adaptive Shield Matrix (MLASM). The architecture ingests real astrophysical telemetry while maintaining bit-exact reproducibility across heterogeneous hardware. All physics operations are executed deterministically, and all cryptographic operations occur exclusively within CPU registers, ensuring complete immunity to leakage and timing jitter.

